Emitting. json-num carries mantissa and decimal places, so
the places are part of the value and survive the trip:
  json-num 2000 3 = 2.000  want 2.000
  json-num 1500 3 = 1.500  want 1.500
  json-num 15 1 = 1.5  want 1.5
  json-num 5 3 = 0.005  want 0.005
  json-num -5 3 = -0.005  want -0.005
  json-num -1500 3 = -1.500  want -1.500
  json-num 7 0 = 7  want 7, no point when there are no places
  json-int 7 = 7  want 7, the control: an integer is untouched

Parsing, from the RFC grammar. A number whose places cancel
against its exponent is an integer and is answered as one, so
a document of plain integers reads exactly as it did before:
  parse 1.5 = 1.5  want 1.5
  parse 2.000 = 2.000  want 2.000
  parse -0.005 = -0.005  want -0.005
  parse 1e3 = 1000  want 1000
  parse 1E3 = 1000  want 1000
  parse 1.5e1 = 15  want 15
  parse 1.5e-1 = 0.15  want 0.15
  parse 15e-3 = 0.015  want 0.015
  parse -7 = -7  want -7, the control
  parse 0 = 0  want 0, the control

Malformed numbers must be refused, not half-read. Each of these
is a production the grammar does not admit:
  1. = refused  want refused, frac needs a digit
  1e = refused  want refused, exp needs a digit
  1e+ = refused  want refused, exp needs a digit after the sign
  .5 = refused  want refused, int is not optional
  1e99 = refused  want refused, past the bound this chapter states

A number reached through the object accessors. json-get-int on a
decimal truncates toward zero rather than answering the default,
because a present numeric field is not a missing one:
  get-int of 2.7 = 2  want 2
  get-int of -2.7 = -2  want -2
  get-int of 5 = 5  want 5, the control

Nesting, so the emitter is exercised through the array and object
walks and not only at the top level:
  array = [2.000,4,-1.5]  want [2.000,4,-1.5]
  object = {"min":0.000,"max":2.000}  want {"min":0.000,"max":2.000}
